WindowPane development ledger

Status: DONE
Milestone: Traceable first-preview and visual-guide development
Dependencies: None
Purpose: Explain actions, effects, reasons, mechanisms and evidence.

Tasks:
- [x] Preserve the rationale for moving from a portal widget to a visual mirror.
- [x] Append implementation and qualification outcomes as they actually occur.

Acceptance criteria:
- Public history contains no credentials, private portal data, or raw conversation.
- Design, implementation, technical verification and human acceptance are distinct.

Completion evidence:
- Native source, build commands, focused tests and actual synthetic UI/pixel
  artifacts exist. See 04_QUALIFICATION.todo for exact observations and limits.

Completion log:
- 2026-11-06: Chose a generic local visual mirror instead of a site client.
  Effect: authentication stays entirely with the source application.
  Reason: the desired utility must not require enterprise sign-in or cookies.
  Mechanism: user-selected Windows capture plus native per-pixel composition.
  Trade-off: the source remains open and responsible for refreshing its data.
- 2026-11-06: Chose explicit pixel composition rather than basic DWM thumbnails.
  Reason: color-keying needs per-pixel alpha, not just a cropped live thumbnail.
  Native capture and CPU readback costs require actual measurements; no promise
  of zero overhead or full application compatibility has been made.
- 2026-11-06: First compilation exposed SDK namespace/header issues and a
  PowerShell argument-parsing issue in the SDK version switch. Qualified the
  C++/WinRT and ABI namespaces, included DWM interop, and quoted the CMake flag.
  The /W4 /WX gate also caught a lossy diagnostic string conversion; UTF-8
  conversion now uses winrt::to_string instead of narrowing wchar_t characters.
- 2026-11-06: First native capture/UI run passed real color-keying and desktop
  blending but timed out after restoring a minimized fixture. Windows had
  queued a differently sized animation frame. A frame's size is not sufficient
  evidence of a true source resize: current normal-window geometry now decides
  invalidation, and transient mismatched frames are discarded.
- 2026-11-06: Actual controls-image inspection found dark checkbox text and an
  incompletely repainted lower section. Native checkbox theming now follows
  the app's explicit colors; the owned-window capture waits for and repaints
  child controls. The new image visibly includes recovery buttons and shortcuts.
- 2026-11-06: Added guarded synthetic integration coverage across snipping,
  color picking, resizing/snap targets, source lifecycle and recovery. The
  harness refuses any non-fixture source HWND before capture and refuses screen
  sample points obscured by unrelated windows.
- 2026-11-06: First preview qualified locally with 18 algorithm cases, 26 native
  integration checks, native startup/tray recovery, formatting and whitespace
  checks. The executable is about 0.4MB, not an installer. A five-second
  180x110 fixture observed about 46MB working set and 0.62% of one logical core;
  these are bounded observations, not promises for every source or GPU.
- 2026-11-06: Added focused chaptered guides, algorithm/design explanations and
  original synthetic figures. No huge book/PDF/atlas, installer, account
  integration, commit, push or release was implied by following the manual.
- 2026-11-06: User requested a detailed README with many GIFs/screenshots. Added
  an explicitly invoked --demo-assets harness that uses only its owned fixture
  and controls, plus a Python/Pillow documentation-only encoder. Four GIFs show
  captured source repaint, opacity events, sampled color removal and aspect fit.
  Nine PNGs show real controls states and annotated native-output references.
  Reading cards/checkerboards are not application chrome; paced animations are
  not mouse recordings or frame-rate/latency measurements.
- 2026-11-06: Normal preview was already running, causing linker LNK1104. Used
  isolated build\media rather than stop the user's app. Extended demo teardown
  initially stalled with active capture; explicitly releasing capture before
  destroying its owned source fixed it. Standard native tests still cover source
  closure separately. Demo export passed 27 checks; both standard CTest targets
  passed. Decoded animation frames change as expected; controls/montage/native
  states and encoded samples visually reviewed. No release or publication.
- 2026-11-06: Encoded-GIF review caught incorrect dark foreground hues because
  the initial shared palette only sampled each sequence's first state. Built
  the palette from all states, re-encoded and reinspected. Every decoded frame's
  native-output area now stays within 12/255 per-channel error of its full-color
  reference; a changing-frame check alone would not have caught the color issue.
- 2026-11-06: User showed the source-resized message and requested an in-place
  recovery action. Added Snip again directly to resized/closed placeholders,
  with pointer hit testing, pressed/cancel behavior, focus and Enter/Space.
  Recovery temporarily overrides click-through and works at compact pane sizes.
  Cancelling must preserve the placeholder instead of reopening an invalid or
  dead source; a typed capture phase now drives that behavior. Fresh capture
  removes recovery chrome. Release build passed 18 pure and 36 native checks;
  actual owned recovery surface was exported and visually inspected.
- 2026-11-06: User clarified that README animations should show a source region
  connected to a floating pane, not only isolated color patches. Authored two
  original diagram animations: select/float/update and move/fade/remove blue
  above another example workspace. Fictional data and windows avoid copying the
  supplied account screenshot. Explicit labels distinguish this artwork from
  actual capture output. Decoded samples inspected; 16/26 distinct GIF states,
  correct loops/duration and all README links verified. Six GIFs and ten PNGs
  now support the guide; no commit, push or publication.
- 2026-11-06: User requested a logo/favicon and a small frosted corner anchor.
  Generated original overlapping-window SVG/PNG/ICO artwork, embedded the icon,
  and used it for native window/tray branding. Added a 28-DIP upper-right grip
  as an owned layered popup: an in-content drawing would disappear with opacity
  or become inaccessible with content-wide click-through. Independent alpha
  and input keep dragging/options usable without restoring a title bar.
- 2026-11-06: Grip follows pane movement/DPI, captures pointer drags, reuses
  snapping and the actual native options menu, and hides with the owner,
  snipping or picking. Final review found a compact-recovery overlap risk;
  the grip now yields when its rectangle intersects the recovery button.
  Tests cover an actual right-button-up-generated menu and unobstructed recovery.
  The frosted look is procedural tint/edge/dots, not sampled desktop or blur.
- 2026-11-06: Warnings-as-errors caught pointer-integer signedness in the icon
  check; matched the API's ULONG_PTR type. An initial icon assertion assumed
  one extraction, but requesting large and small handles returned two locally.
  The corrected assertion requires successful extraction and both real handles.
  Release passes 18 pure and 44 native checks; optional demo passes 43 and
  ordinary startup/tray/recovery/exit smoke passes.
- 2026-11-06: Added native grip-surface/relative-geometry evidence and updated
  both drawn animations to show the handle. README uses the original logo and
  actual combined grip/pane reference, with explicit content-only GIF provenance.
  Native controls/logo/grip and decoded illustration samples visually inspected;
  all 41 decoded native GIF areas stay within 5/255 channel error. Nine ICO
  sizes decode; formatting, parsing, whitespace and local links pass.
  No new runtime dependency, private capture, commit, install or publication.
- 2026-11-06: Final lifecycle review found that default WM_CLOSE could destroy
  just the grip; it now hides/releases the pane while retaining the handle for
  recovery. Native close/show coverage passes. A final ordinary startup smoke
  exposed a separate readiness-check race: PowerShell casts IntPtr.Zero to
  true, so the script could accept an unready zero HWND after 100ms. Explicit
  IntPtr.Zero comparisons now honor its existing ten-second wait. The corrected
  startup/tray/recovery/exit smoke passes; only its own process was cleaned up.
- 2026-11-06: User requested 60 fps by default, or the maximum. Added 30/60 fps
  alongside 1/5/10, shared the allowed-rate list between capture and controls,
  and defaulted to its highest value. Capture intervals now use native
  100-nanosecond units rather than truncating 30/60 fps to whole milliseconds.
  Windows UI timers remain best-effort; neither fresh source pixels nor a
  measured 60 fps output rate is guaranteed.
- 2026-11-06: New-session UI selection and actual 30/60 fps changes deliver
  repainted fixture pixels. Release passes 18 pure/47 native, optional demo
  46, startup/tray/recovery/exit smoke, formatting/links/media decoding.
  The bounded resource observation explicitly selects 5 fps for comparability,
  then restores the 60 fps default before controls export. Refreshed controls
  visibly show 60 fps / maximum. No private capture, commit or publication.
- 2026-11-06: User requested a release. GitHub reported read-only repository
  access; user selected local files for manual upload rather than changing
  authentication. Prepared v0.1.0-preview.1 EXE/portable ZIP/source snapshot,
  SHA256SUMS, release notes and explicit manual-publication instructions.
  Portable EXE bytes match the qualified 60 fps build; x64 and unsigned status
  confirmed. Archives pass CRC/entry-byte checks and exclude private context,
  git internals, caches and raw build output.
- 2026-11-06: Source features are still uncommitted, so a tag at the old branch
  head would misrepresent them. Included a hashed public working-tree snapshot
  and required matching source commit/push before tagging in the manual guide.
  No GitHub release, tag, commit, push, signing, install or upload was performed.
- 2026-11-06: User requested GitHub release automation and explicitly selected
  manual workflow runs only. Added a Windows x64 build/pixel-test/packaging job
  and a separate contents-write publisher. Action versions/inputs were verified
  from public provider metadata and pinned to commits. Hosted image manifest
  lists the required VS2022/SDK; native capture/UI checks remain local.
- 2026-11-06: Release input is validated and passed through environment values.
  New tags are created at the exact checked-out/built SHA, never overwritten.
  Publication uses a draft, downloads/checks uploaded assets, then publishes
  an unsigned preview. Failures can leave a tag/draft for manual inspection.
  Source/workflow must first be committed/pushed by an authorized maintainer.
- 2026-11-06: Actual workflow Windows blocks passed default/custom/invalid tag
  cases, build, 18 pixel checks and ZIP content/hash checks. GNU checksum review
  caught Windows CRLF being interpreted as part of Linux filenames. Explicit
  LF-only output fixes the manifest; both assets now pass GNU sha256sum.
  actionlint, publisher Bash syntax, docs links and whitespace checks pass.
  No hosted execution, account switch, push, tag or release publication.
